   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 8.1. Increased capacity of member States to develop new or reformed sustainable national social protection strategies, policies or legal frameworks to extend coverage and enhance benefit adequacy</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Government implemented  measures through several Presidential Decrees in July and August 2020 and August 2021 to mitigate the COVID-19 impact on the most vulnerable and workers who lost their jobs. This included a) the extension of social assistance benefits from 700,000 to 1.25 million families, b) the increase in the unemployment benefits  to a minimum of 50% of the average wage at the former workplace, but no less than the minimum national wage; c)  assessment of groups of informal workers who could join the existing unemployment insurance scheme; and d) strengthening of the  capacity of 50 staff in the Ministry of Finance, Ministry of Employment and Labour Relations and the Pensions Fund to manage social protection policy design and delivery.
 ILO contributed by:
i. facilitating the participatory process with republican working group (consisting of ministers and deputy ministers) and five thematic working groups and social partners (terms of reference, briefing notes and meeting minutes available upon request)
ii. preparing technical reports analysing the social protection system, schemes, impact of COVID-19, costing and fiscal space identification, which resulted in the extension of benefits, extension of coverage and improvement of delivery;
iii. in capacity development of constituents to apply social security standards into policy making (10 webinars)
iv. preparing policy briefs and infographics on the extension of unemployment insurance coverage.


The UN Joint Programme also supported introduction of Single Registry for social protection system and institutional reform &#x2013; identification and creation of single entity to manage social protection system.</narrative>
   </description>
