   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The National Gender Equality Strategy was adopted by the Parliament on 29 May 2021. A National Strategy for Social Protection until 2030 and costed three-year Action Plan expected to be adopted by the end of 2021 includes costing of community based care services, a system with care-givers having the status of self-employed with registered work experience. Constituents analysed national maternity protection legislation in order to align it with Maternity Protection Convention No. 183. 1. The ILO commissioned the development of the national report &quot;Women in the World of Work&#x201d;, which offered a range of policy options. Relevant options were included in gender equality, social protection and employment programmes. 

3. The ILO facilitated the process of drafting national strategy for social protection, provided technical backstopping with due regards to social security and gender equality standards for 5 Thematic Working Groups drafting the strategy to National social protection strategy. It has provided analysis of the budget and costing od social protection schemes and services.  Action Plan that accompanies national social protection strategy contains specific costed measures for support services for disadvantaged women and persons with disabilities entering labour market, care economy and community based social services designed with ILO&#x2019;s support.

4. The ILO provided technical support to strengthen the capacity of 15 Parliamentary Gender Equality Commission members and 40 social partner representatives to review national legislation from gender equality perspective, conduct gender responsive budgeting, maternity protection and improve gender disaggregated statistical data collection. The following training sessions were conducted: (i) Gender responsive budgeting; (ii) Gender audit of labour laws; (iii) Key principles and practices on maternity protection; (iv) Equal pay for work of equal value; (v) Human interest story: Career or Maternity: Both!.

5. The ILO organised technical capacity building webinars on gender responsive budgeting for 35 staff of the Ministry of Mahalla and Family Affairs which is responsible for implementation of all gender equality measures in different sectoral policies (social protection and employment) as well as for Gender Equality Strategy.

